Why: three changes to the dec/enc close paths.

They have the same SIGKILL leak shape as 0210 - interruptible device locks plus
early returns on firmware finish/timeout failures skip the buffer frees,
permanently leaking the instance's allocations from the fixed pool (the fd is
gone either way, so bailing out converts a firmware hiccup into a leak). Locks
made uninterruptible, buffers always freed, VPU stop/reset ordered before the
frees.

Bailing out without a successful quiesce (siblings live, or the last-instance
safety reset failed) leaves the firmware still DMAing into the instance's
buffers, so those paths mark the instance keep_dma_bufs (0250) and
wave5_cleanup_instance (0210) leaks them rather than freeing them.

Also disables decoder reordering (reorder_enable=FALSE, the vendor's DEC_PIC
configuration): the low-latency no-B streams need no reorder delay.

Live rate changes are marked pending by the V4L2 control handlers (0280) and
applied from start_encode rather than from the ioctl, because the firmware only
accepts a parameter change between pictures. wave5_vpu_enc_update_rate() syncs
the open-param copy the change command reads from, and
wave5_vpu_enc_apply_pending_rate() clears the pending mask before it attempts the
change, so a change that fails cannot re-arm itself on every following frame.

Firmware reload. wave5_initialize_vpu() drops the wave5_vpu_is_init() guard and
wave5_vpu_recover() wraps a block reset plus a fresh firmware download around it.
The guard reads W5_VCPU_CUR_PC and treats any running VCPU as already
initialised, so it rejects exactly the case that needs the download - a firmware
that has stopped answering - and the wave5_vpu_re_init() fallback it takes
instead is a no-op here, because its old_code_base check compares equal against
the page-granular first-fit coherent pool (0300).

Probe forces too. Nothing on this SoC powers the codec block down between a
module unload and the next load, or across a watchdog reset, so W5_VCPU_CUR_PC
is still non-zero and the guard rejected the probe with -EBUSY: the recorded
"vpu_init_with_bitcode, fail: -16" warm reload failure, which made a module swap
impossible and every driver change a full reflash. A cold probe reaches the same
reset-and-download path either way, because a VCPU that has never run reads back
a program counter of zero, so this cannot change cold-boot behaviour.

Both close paths call wave5_vpu_recover_locked() in place of SW_RESET_SAFETY. SAFETY routes through
wave5_vpu_sleep_wake(), whose wake handshake does not complete on this
integration and which opens with wave5_wait_vpu_busy(), the same wait that fails
when the command queue is jammed - so it returned -ETIMEDOUT before reaching the
block reset and the frees it was meant to make safe never happened.
SW_RESET_ON_BOOT asserts W5_RST_BLOCK_ALL directly. The reset is global, so it
stays behind the existing last-instance check.

